{"chunk_id":"R4-2513321-001","collection":"ran4_tdoc_chunks","metadata":{"source":["OPPO"],"tdocNumber":"R4-2513321","title":"RAN4 operation efficiency"},"point_id":"6768367833497260072"}
en it. This makes the specification maintenance and reading quite difficult. This kind of specs should be avoided in 6G. Some approaches can be considered in 6G to avoid such unnecessary difficulties. Take 38.133 as example, currently it includes both the core requirements, performance requirements and test cases. The total spec is over 5700 pages, and the core requirements + performance requirements is around 1500 pages, test cases over 4100 pages. This unnecessarily complex the specification. It might be more reasonable to split the spec into two or three specs covering core requirements, performance requirements, and tests. Proposal 1: Avoid defining too huge spec like 38.133, and consider splitting the 6G RRM spec into several specs covering core requirements, performance requirements, and test cases respectively. Spec modernization Regarding the modernization, there are some discussions in other working groups like RAN2 and SA on the problems identified when using word-based specification. Besides, RAN2 has discussed some tools like markdown to facilitate the ASN.1 management. Now it is still at early stage of this kind of specification modernization. RAN4 may also be involved at some stage. RAN4 need to keep an eye on the discussions in other groups. Observation 1: In other working group, there are some preliminary discussions on the problems identified of using word-based specification, and also potential alternative tools. Proposal 2: RAN4 needs to keep an eye on the progress of alternative tools to replace current word-based specification, and prepare for the potential impacts. New tools for band/band combinations Band combination database has been discussed in the past few meetings and the intention is to remove the huge number of band combinations in current specification. This will help to simplify the RAN4 specification, and simplify the band combination handling like cross check of the fallbacks and specified/missing band combinations. Therefore, in general, this is good to improve RAN4 6G work. Meanwhile, fully incorporate this new tool into RAN4 specifications and processes may still need some trials before fully switch to this new tool. Observation 2: Band combination database is useful in simplifying RAN4 specifications and facilitate the band combination handling. Proposal 3: RAN4 to make some trial of using this band combination database and study how to incorporate this database into RAN4 specification and meeting handling like CR process, etc. CR operation NWM usage The number of CRs to be handled in each RAN4 meeting is huge as can be seen in below statistic figure. And if further consider the revisions during the meeting, then the number will be even larger. This trend may be continued in early 6G. How to manage the CR efficiently will directly impact the RAN4 operation efficiency. RAN4 has made big progress in recent meetings by using the NWM and flag process before meeting. This can trigger early offline discussions and revisions which saves much meeting time. The NWM can continue to be used in 6G. Proposal 4: Continue to use NWM flag process to trigger early offline discussion and revision in 6G. Cover page issues However, it has been noticed that there are many CRs revised during the meeting because of the CR cover page format issue. There are some discussions on the possibility of introducing some new tools to automatically generate the CR cover page and avoid such unnecessary efforts. Proposal 5: Study the possibility of introducing some new tools to automatically generate the CR cover page and avoid unnecessary efforts on the cover page format issues. Mirror CRs Another point is the handling of mirror CRs. In each RAN4 meeting, there are large number of mirror CRs for the later releases. Sometimes the mirror CRs

